(* RoboLang v1.7 grammar, implemented in src/robolang/parser.py.
   RoboLang 1.6 plus region limits (decision 0039). Differences from grammar-v1.6.ebnf are marked [1.7].
   Everything not shown here is as in grammar-v1.6.ebnf (and grammar-v1.5.ebnf and those below it).
   Normative text, diagnostics and static rules: spec/robolang-v1.7.md *)

version_decl  = "robolang" , NUMBER , ";" ;                (* [1.7] NUMBER written exactly 1.7 *)

annotation    = "@safety" , safety_rule , ";" ;
safety_rule   = "max_speed" , NUMBER , [ SPEED_UNIT ]
              | region_limit ;                              (* [1.7] *)
region_limit  = ( "no_go_region" | "in_region" ) , NAME ;   (* [1.7] NAME is a region parameter of the task *)

(* [1.7] "no_go_region" and "in_region" have been reserved words since 1.1, so there is no new word.
   In a 1.7 file they are accepted after "@safety" only; "move r to x in_region c" is still E211.
   [1.7] Static rules the grammar does not express (spec/robolang-v1.7.md section 3):
   - the NAME is a parameter of the task of type region;
   - a region is named by one region limit only: not twice, not as both no_go_region and in_region (E323);
   - a task has at most 8 region limits (E324).
   Region limits and max_speed annotations may be mixed in any order, before "pre", "goal" and "plan".
   [1.7] Meaning (spec/robolang-v1.7.md sections 2 and 6):
   - a region limit holds for the whole task: every "move" of every robot, on every path, handlers included. There is
     no limit on one step, on an arm, on a distance to a person or on a force; those cannot be written.
   - it is a request that is checked against the world before the run and never enforced. A world may list, for a
     region, the locations it contains, and for a robot the kinds of region limit it honours. The run fails before any
     step if a robot that moves does not honour the kind (S003), if a target (a "move" or "place" location or a "robot_at"
     goal) is inside a no_go_region or outside an in_region whose locations are listed (S004), or if a robot that
     moves starts there (S005). Such a request is infeasible, not inexpressible.
   - "delegate" and Core carry no region limits: a task with one cannot be delegated (X605).
   - a limit the request asks for must be written, since a program without it passes every other check; a limit the
     request did not ask for must not be added. "wait until region_clear c" and "if region_clear c" are not limits. *)

(* Reserved words in a 1.7 file: those of grammar-v1.6.ebnf. *)
